WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:05.400
This year, the average American&nbsp;
will spend nearly $600 on gift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.000
cards — more than all other gifts combined.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:18.180
Naturally, a few of those 198 billion dollars&nbsp;
will go unused. Cards get lost in the couch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.640
forgotten entirely, or a few pennies&nbsp;
here and there left as remainder.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.800
Individually, these financial&nbsp;
leftovers are insignificant.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.040
But collectively, they add up.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:40.980
Starbucks, now as much a bank as a coffee shop,&nbsp;
held $181 million worth of stored credit in 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.980
And when Radio Shack declared bankruptcy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.060
it reported $46 million worth&nbsp;
of unredeemed gift cards.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.540
Where does all that money go?

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.380
Common sense would say: the company’s pockets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:01.200
of course. Why else would gift cards be placed&nbsp;
in prominent, high-traffic parts of the store?

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:09.600
But… not necessarily. Quite often, unused&nbsp;
gift cards are commandeered by the state.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.240
And not just any state. Not,&nbsp;
as you might reasonably assume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.820
the state where the card was purchased.&nbsp;
Nor, even, the home state of its buyer.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.860
In reality, a significant portion&nbsp;
of all unclaimed gift cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.000
across the entire United States,&nbsp;
regardless of where they were purchased,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.880
are seized by just one state — the&nbsp;
second-smallest state, in fact — Delaware.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.580
And its tiny population of one&nbsp;
million profits enormously.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.080
Delaware is one of five states with no sales tax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.380
along with Alaska, Oregon,&nbsp;
Montana, and New Hampshire.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.940
It also has one of the lowest&nbsp;
property taxes in the country.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.780
Yet, despite collecting lower overall taxes&nbsp;
than any state except resource-rich Alaska,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.160
Delaware spends more per-capita&nbsp;
than all but three others.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:11.040
In other words, Delaware operates in&nbsp;
many ways like a tiny, profit-seeking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:18.300
rent-maximizing, oil-rich emirate. Rather than&nbsp;
being blessed with a natural resource like oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:24.480
it’s been granted the near-exclusive legal right&nbsp;
to collect many of America’s unused gift cards.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.140
Sponsored by Nebula, the exclusive home&nbsp;
to my Original series “China, Actually”.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.960
Delaware is not the only&nbsp;
place that seizes unclaimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.680
property. All 50 states have laws of this variety.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.320
When someone dies without heirs, for instance,&nbsp;
the state takes custody of their home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.260
If no rightful owner is found, it’s sold and&nbsp;
the proceeds redistributed to the public.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:58.620
But this process, called “escheatment”, was&nbsp;
envisioned with physical property in mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.080
As wealth became increasingly&nbsp;
intangible, things got complicated.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.400
In 1965, four different states tried to claim the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.640
same undeposited paychecks&nbsp;
from Sun Oil worth $26,000.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.640
Texas, where the checks were issued,&nbsp;
conveniently argued the state which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.120
had the “most significant contacts&nbsp;
with the debt” should receive them.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.020
New Jersey, where the company&nbsp;
was legally incorporated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.300
argued it was entitled to the profits.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.720
Because Sun Oil was headquartered in Pennsylvania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.460
it too made a play for the money, as the&nbsp;
debtor’s “principal place of business”.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.460
Finally, Florida's claim was based on the&nbsp;
last-known address of the paycheck recipients.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.080
The Supreme Court was charged with&nbsp;
deciding which of these rules to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.460
sanctify with its gavel and pretend&nbsp;
wasn’t as arbitrary as the next.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.300
In the end, the court did what courts are good at:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.560
concealing the arbitrary nature&nbsp;
of its ruling with complexity.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.120
The fairest solution, it&nbsp;
said, would be a compromise.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:08.520
First, unclaimed property would be&nbsp;
assigned based, as Florida suggested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:13.980
on the recipient’s last-known address. In&nbsp;
the unlikely event none could be found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.900
it would simply revert to where the company&nbsp;
was incorporated — in this case, New Jersey.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:25.260
But what the court failed to anticipate&nbsp;
was the monumental rise of gift cards.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.680
Almost by definition, no one knows the&nbsp;
last-known address of the owner of a gift card.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.000
After all, most gift cards&nbsp;
are… gifted to someone else.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.520
In practice, therefore, unused&nbsp;
gift cards almost always belong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.800
to the state in which the company is registered.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.760
And that state is almost always Delaware.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.780
This is the number of Fortune 500 companies&nbsp;
registered in each state as of 2018.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.480
Virginia has 11, Ohio, 16, and New York, 20.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:06.720
Now, watch what happens when we&nbsp;
add Delaware, at well over 300.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:13.740
Delaware is to corporations what Liberia,&nbsp;
Panama, and the Marshall Islands are to ships.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.260
…The only difference is that Liberia, Panama,&nbsp;
and the Marshall Islands have competition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:26.400
Whereas, 95% of businesses that incorporate&nbsp;
outside their home state choose Delaware.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.780
The first and most obvious reason is money.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:37.200
Delaware doesn’t tax “intangible investments”&nbsp;
like trademarks and patents, allowing companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.700
to transfer their mascots, logos, and&nbsp;
jingles to the state and save millions.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.460
The second is speed.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.480
An interaction with the Delaware&nbsp;
Division of Corporations would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.320
blow the mind of anyone with the&nbsp;
misfortune of having visited a DMV.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:58.800
If you’re willing to pay extra, you can form&nbsp;
your company in less than 30 minutes. And its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:04.740
offices stay open until midnight, making life&nbsp;
easy for anyone in Dubai, Lagos, or Hong Kong.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:08.460
Then, there’s the extreme anonymity.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.080
You don’t need to prove your identity&nbsp;
to register a business in Delaware.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:17.580
In fact, you don’t have to provide&nbsp;
your name, address, or phone number.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:24.300
All the state requires is a single “contact&nbsp;
person”, who can be anyone 18 or older who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.100
lives on planet Earth — whether or not they’re&nbsp;
a U.S. Citizen or even live in the country.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:36.420
In theory, any state in the Union could offer&nbsp;
companies anonymity, speed, and low taxes.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:43.500
But there’s one thing only Delaware has:&nbsp;
decades and decades of legal precedent.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:51.300
The United States inherited from England two types&nbsp;
of courts: Common Law and Chancery — the latter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.720
which dealt with issues of fairness,&nbsp;
especially in the realm of property.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.380
Since then, most states have merged the two into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:04.140
one “Superior” court. Except three —&nbsp;
Tennessee, Mississippi, and Delaware.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:10.140
Today, the Delaware Chancery Court is effectively&nbsp;
a separate justice system for corporations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:14.040
Instead of erratic juries, its decisions are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:18.000
made by specialized judges who’ve&nbsp;
seen thousands of similar cases.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:23.160
In other words, corporations choose&nbsp;
Delaware for its predictability.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:29.160
Now, it may sound like the State of Delaware is&nbsp;
effectively colluding with Corporate America.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:31.740
But… not exactly.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.580
Sure, Delaware fights hard to attract companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.860
But, at the same time, it’s hardly any&nbsp;
less profit-oriented than they are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.080
Once they’ve set up shop, it extracts&nbsp;
from them every last penny it can.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:53.100
Needless to say, this near-monopoly on corporate&nbsp;
registration is extraordinarily profitable.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:08:00.060
It collects more in corporate fees alone than&nbsp;
42 states collect in corporate income taxes.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.520
But the State of Delaware is far&nbsp;
from satisfied. It wants more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.780
And that puts it on a collision&nbsp;
course with nearly everyone else.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.840
Take gift cards, for instance.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.400
Delaware interprets the 1965 Supreme Court ruling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:21.420
as a license to collect virtually&nbsp;
all of America’s unused gift cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:26.760
And it will aggressively fight any company&nbsp;
or even government that stands in its way.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:34.560
Remember, the purpose of escheatment is, first and&nbsp;
foremost, to reunite lost property with its owner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:39.000
The state is a mere custodian&nbsp;
until a rightful heir is found.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.700
Because it’s nearly impossible to track&nbsp;
down the rightful heir of a gift card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.760
many states have quite sensibly exempted them.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:54.180
But it doesn’t matter. Delaware still claims&nbsp;
that money as its own — even when the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:59.100
in question has not a single office,&nbsp;
employee, or customer within its borders.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:03.540
In other words, the States of&nbsp;
California, Texas, and New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:08.940
for example, can’t decide what happens to the&nbsp;
unused gift cards sold in their own territory.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:13.980
It’s as if Delaware could impose&nbsp;
an extraterritorial income tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.980
on residents of Nevada, where there isn’t one.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:22.320
Its motive, of course, is revenue. Lots of it.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.260
In 2001, the state discovered that less than 1%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.280
of companies had reported unclaimed&nbsp;
property and decided to crack down.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.680
It outsourced the enforcement of this&nbsp;
arcane law to four auditing firms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:40.380
These auditors, in turn, would reach out&nbsp;
to companies with a wildly-high “estimate”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:45.960
of what they owed. If the company couldn’t&nbsp;
disprove this enormous number with 20 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.320
worth of documentation, it would&nbsp;
be forced to cough up the money.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:55.920
While the State of Washington keeps its unclaimed&nbsp;
property forever and even has a staff of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:01.020
searching for its rightful owner, only&nbsp;
about 2% is ever returned in Delaware.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:04.020
The rest, it simply spends.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:08.640
Unclaimed property is Delaware’s&nbsp;
third-largest source of revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.600
behind only personal income&nbsp;
taxes and corporate fees.

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:18.060
In 2016, a federal judge ruled that&nbsp;
the state of Delaware was, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:20.940
“engaged in a game of ‘gotcha’&nbsp;
that shocks the conscience”.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:27.540
The victims in this game are not only&nbsp;
corporations but the entire United States.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:33.840
By delegating to states the power to register&nbsp;
corporations with no minimum standards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.020
the U.S. federal government&nbsp;
unleashed a race to the bottom.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:42.120
…A race that would ultimately benefit&nbsp;
one state to the detriment of all others.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:47.760
Then, in 1965, the Supreme Court further&nbsp;
tipped the scales in Delaware’s favor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.440
by unintentionally granting it a virtual&nbsp;
monopoly on unclaimed corporate property.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:55.740
Recall that its rationale, at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:01.980
was “fairness” to every state. The court didn’t&nbsp;
foresee how Delaware would weaponize its ruling.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.860
And that’s how we got here.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:11.460
Today, a state home to just 0.3% of&nbsp;
the U.S. population effectively sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:16.680
U.S. corporate law — a power it used&nbsp;
to propel the country to #1 on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:22.020
“Financial Secrecy Index” — above Switzerland,&nbsp;
Luxembourg, and the British Virgin Islands.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:28.260
Thanks to Delaware, the United States of&nbsp;
America may be the best place to launder money.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.500
Viktor Bout, one of the world’s most&nbsp;
notorious arms traffickers and inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:39.480
for the 2005 film “Lord of War” set up&nbsp;
dozens of shell companies in Delaware.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:43.680
The state became a sanctuary&nbsp;
for the profits he made selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.860
weapons used in wars throughout Afghanistan, Iran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:53.220
and Syria — years-long and absolutely brutal&nbsp;
conflicts which remain poorly understood today.

